against Escherichia coli and Staphylococcus aureus. Recently, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) nanoparticles have attracted broad interest on account of their convenient synthesis [21], tunable functionalities and promising application in proton conduction [22], catalysis [23], magnetic refrigeration [24], and drug delivery [25]. MOFs are constructed by metal ions and organic linkers, and their inorganic-organic hybrid nature usually brings about good affinity with polymers [26]. Recently, investigation on the utilization of MOFs for the development of biopolymer-based functional nanocomposites has aroused great interest. Mubashir et al. [27] reported that incorporating amine functionalized MOF \(\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) -MIL-53) into CA matrix resulted in nanocomposites with excellent plasticization resistance and gas separation performance. Rickhoff et al. [28] investigated the thermal, adsorption, and antimicrobial properties of the CA/MOFs nanocomposites. Nevertheless, to the best of our knowledge, there are no reports concerning the use of MOFs as nano-fillers to create CA-based functional nanocomposite films applicable to transparent packaging and UV-protection areas.

Therefore, the main objective of this study is to prepare CA-based functional nanocomposite films by exploiting MOFs as nano-fillers. Herein, flower-like Cu-MOF nanoparticles were synthesized and blended with CA by solution-blending to fabricate the CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposites. The morphological, mechanical, thermal, surface hydrophobicity, water vapor barrier, cytotoxicity, photostability and UV-shielding characteristics of as fabricated nanocomposite films were thoroughly evaluated. This is the first report on the exploitation of MOFs as nano-fillers to develop CA-based nanocomposites with improved physical and functional properties and broaden their applications in transparent packaging and UV-protection areas.

# 2. Experimental section

# 2.1. Materials and chemicals

Cellulose acetate (CA, CAS: 9004-35-7, acetyl content: \(39.8\;\mathrm{wt}\%\) , hydroxyl content: \(3.5\,\mathrm{Wt}\%\) ) was purchased from Aladdin Industrial Corporation (Shanghai, China). Analytical grade reagents 2-methylimidazole (2MI), copper(II) chloride dihydrate \(\mathrm{CuCl}_{2}\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}0\) , hexadecyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB), Rhodamine B (RhB) and titanium oxide \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) , \(25\;\mathrm{nm}\) ) were also obtained from Aladdin Industrial Corporation. Acetone and ethanol were supplied by Xilong Chemical Co. (China).

# 2.2. Synthesis of flower-like Cu-MOF nanoparticles

Cu-MOF nanoparticles were synthesized via a conventional aqueous solution method according to the literature [29]. \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) of 2MI aqueous solution (1 M) and \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) of CTAB aqueous solution (1 mM) were respectively added into \(10\,\mathrm{mL}\) of \(\mathsf{C u C l}_{2}\cdot2\mathsf{H}_{2}0\) aqueous solution \((0.05\;\mathrm{M})\) , followed by gently stirring for 1 min. Then, the solution remained undisturbed for \(45\,\mathrm{min}\) at room temperature. The obtained brownish powder was centrifuged, washed with ethanol for 5 times, and then dried at \(65\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.\)

# 2.3. Film preparation

1 g of CA powder was dissolved in \(15\,\mathrm{mL}\) acetone with magnetic stirring at \(1500\,\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(2\,\mathrm{h}\) at room temperature. Simultaneously, a certain amount of Cu-MOF nanoparticles were dispersed in acetone to form a homogeneous dispersion, which was then added into the CA solution with stirring for \(1\,\mathrm{h}\) . Subsequently, the uniform mixtures were casted on flat glass dishes and dried in a vacuum oven at \(30\,^{\circ}C\) for \(48\,\mathrm{h}\) . Finally, polymer films with the thickness around \(100\,\upmu\mathrm{m}\) were peeled off the dishes for further characterization. The obtained CA/Cu-MOF films containing 1.0, 3.0, 5.0 and \(7.0\,\mathrm{wt}\%\,\mathrm{Cu}\) -MOF were denoted as CA/Cu-MOF1,0, CA/Cu-MOF3,0, CA/Cu-MOF5,0 and CA/Cu-MOF7,0, respectively.

# 2.4. Characterization

# 2.4.1. X-ray diffraction (XRD)

XRD characterization was performed on a Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer with Cu Kα radiation (40 kV, 40 mA) at a scan rate of \(5^{\circ}\) \(\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) .

# 2.4.2. Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR)

FTIR spectra were recorded with a Nicolet NEXUS-470 FTIR spectrometer from 400 to \(4000\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) at a resolution of \(4\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) . All the samples were carefully dried in vacuum oven at \(80\,^{\circ}C\) for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) before FTIR testing to reduce the effects from humidity/water in the samples.

# 2.4.3. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM)

The morphologies of Cu-MOF and the cryo-fractured surfaces of CA/ Cu-MOF films were evaluated via a field emission scanning electron microscope (SU-5000, Hitachi) equipped with an energy dispersive X-ray (EDX) spectrometer.

# 2.4.4. Differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) analysis

DSC measurements were conducted under nitrogen using a DSC Q20 apparatus (TA Instruments) in the temperature range from 30 to \(240\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) at \(10\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) .

# 2.4.5. Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA)

TGA measurements were carried out on a Perkin-Elmer TGA7 equipment under a nitrogen environment with a \(10\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) heating rate from 25 to \(800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) .

# 2.4.6. Mechanical properties

Tensile tests were performed by an Instron1121 machine with a test speed of \(10\,\mathrm{mm\,min}^{-1}\) at room temperature according to GB/T 1040- 2006. For each sample, at least five specimens were measured to calculate the mean value.

# 2.4.7. Surface wettability and water vapor permeability

The surface wettability of films was evaluated by using a static contact angle analyzer (SZ-CAMB1). The water contact angle of each sample was obtained from the average of three measurements.

The water vapor permeability (WVP) of films was estimated according to the standard method E96-E95 (ASTM method 1995) with a slight modification [30,31]. Glass cups with \(4\:\mathrm{g}\) of \(\mathsf{C a C l}_{2}\) were covered with sample films, and then weighted and placed in a humidity chamber with temperature of \(38\,^{\circ}C\) and relative humidity (RH) of \(90\%\) . Cups were weighed every hour for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) . Water vapor transmission rate \((W V T R,\mathrm{g}\,\mathrm{m}^{-2}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1})\) and \(W V P\,(\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{Pa}^{-1})\) were calculated as:

\[
WVTR=\frac{\Delta W}{\Delta t\times A}
\]

\[
WVP=\frac{WVTR}{P(R1-R2)}\times L
\]

where \(\Delta W/\Delta t\) is the rate of weight change \((\mathrm{g/h}),A\) is the test area \((\mathfrak{m}^{2}),L\) is the film thickness (m), \(P\) is the saturation vapor pressure \(\left(\mathrm{Pa}\right)\) of water at the test temperature \((38\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) , R1 and \(R2\) are the relative humidity in the humidity chamber ( \(90\%\) RH) and in the glass cups \((0\%\) RH), respectively.

# 2.4.8. Ultraviolet-visible (UV–vis) analysis

UV–vis absorbance and transmittance spectra were collected using a Lamdba 750 UV–vis spectrophotometer from 200 to \(800\,\mathrm{nm}\) .

# 2.4.9. UV-shielding test

UV-shielding performance of sample films was estimated according to a referenced method [32], via analyzing the degradation behavior of
RhB solution \((1 \times 10^{-5}\,\mathrm{M}, 50\,\mathrm{mL})\) with \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) \((100\,\mathrm{mg})\) under UV light \((365\,\mathrm{nm})\) irradiation produced from a high-pressure mercury lamp \((250\,\mathrm{W})\). Prior to irradiation, the RhB solution was put into a single-necked flask and covered with a pure CA film or a CA/Cu-MOF composite film. The distance from the mercury lamp to the film was about \(25\,\mathrm{cm}\). The absorbance \(A_{0}\) at \(552\,\mathrm{nm}\) of RhB solution before UV radiation, and the corresponding absorbance \(A_{\mathrm{t}}\) of RhB solution after a certain irradiation time (t) was respectively measured by a Lamdba \(750\,\mathrm{UV}\)-vis spectrometer. The value of \(I = A_{\mathrm{t}}/A_{0} \times 100\%\) was calculated and used for assessing the UV-shielding performance.

# 2.4.10. Photostability of the films

The photostability of sample films was assessed according to a referenced method [33]. After the continuous exposure of strong UV light \((365\,\mathrm{nm})\) irradiation for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) from a high-pressure mercury lamp \((250\,\mathrm{W})\), the UV–vis transmittance spectra of the irradiated sample films was measured. The distance from the mercury lamp to the film was about \(25\,\mathrm{cm}\).

# 2.4.11. Cytotoxicity evaluation

The cytotoxicity of CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite films was evaluated based on the viability of normal human liver cells (HL-7702) according to ISO 10993-5 standard. First, sample films \((2\,\mathrm{mm} \times 2\,\mathrm{mm})\) were well sterilized by exposing to UV rays. When the number of cells in the culture reached \(2.5 \times 10^{5}\,\mathrm{cell/mL}\), sample films were transferred to 96-well microbiological plates and sufficient cells along with the culture medium were added to them. The above treated HL-7702 cells were then incubated at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) and \(5\%\) of \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) atmosphere. After \(48\,\mathrm{h}\), cells were washed with PBS solution and \(10\,\upmu\mathrm{L}\) MTT solution \((5\,\mathrm{mg/mL}\) in PBS) was poured on the cell layer. The plates were then placed in a dark environment for \(4\,\mathrm{h}\) inside the incubator. The formazan crystals were dissolved with DMSO \((100\,\upmu\mathrm{L})\) and the optical density (OD) at \(400\,\mathrm{nm}\) was measured using a BioTek Cytation 5 plate reader. Cell viability was calculated by dividing the OD value of sample group by the OD value of control group [34].

