As a typical zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8), the as-synthesized Cu-MOF crystallizes into the zeolite sodalite topology, in which each bivalent Cu cation joins four 2-methylimidazole (2MI), forming a resistant structure with large cages (see the inset of Fig. 1a) [35]. Fig. 1a displays the SEM image of the as-synthesized Cu-MOF crystals. It can be observed that the Cu-MOF crystals exhibit uniform flower shape (termed as flower-like) with distinct wrinkles on the surface similar to a petal structure. The average diameter of flower-like Cu-MOF is around \(260\,\mathrm{nm}\). Fig. 1b shows the representative XRD patterns of the as-synthesized Cu-MOF, CA and CA/Cu-MOF films. For Cu-MOF, all peaks are in accord with previous reports [29], verifying that the synthesized Cu-MOF is pure phase. Furthermore, one sharp intense peak at around \(2\theta = 14.1^{\circ}\) can be observed, revealing high crystallinity for the synthesized Cu-MOF. For pure CA, one weak diffraction peak is observed at approximately \(2\theta = 8.7^{\circ}\), which is identified as the main characteristic of semi-crystalline acetylated cellulose [36]. After dispersing Cu-MOF into the CA matrix, the XRD patterns of CA/Cu-MOF composites display no distinct Cu-MOF characteristic peak yet only the CA diffraction peak from CA, suggesting the good compatibility between Cu-MOF and CA and the unaltered crystal structure compared with CA.

To investigate the interaction between Cu-MOF and CA, FTIR spectra of Cu-MOF, CA and CA/Cu-MOF films were measured as displayed in Fig. 2. For pure Cu-MOF, the band at approximately \(2921\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is assigned to \(-\mathrm{CH}\) stretching of methyl groups, and the bands at 1415 and \(1308\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are due to \(-\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{N}\) stretching vibration [29]. With regard to CA/Cu-MOF films, the broad absorption peak at about \(3478\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is assigned to \(-\mathrm{OH}\) stretching vibration, the band at \(2942-2920\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) corresponds to \(-\mathrm{CH}\) stretching vibration and increases with Cu-MOF addition, and the peak at \(1747\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is attributed to \(-\mathrm{C} = 0\) stretching vibration [19]. The bands at \(1431\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1370\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) correspond to

