# 3.2. Mechanical properties

The typical stress–strain curves, tensile strength, and elongation at break of neat CA and CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite films are presented in Fig. 4. The neat CA film displays a tensile strength of \(35.5\;\mathrm{MPa}\) and elongation at break of \(1.6\%\) . With the addition of \(1.0\,\mathrm{wt}\%\) Cu-MOF, the tensile strength and elongation at break are increased to \(45.7\;\mathrm{MPa}\) and \(2.4\%\) , respectively. On increasing the amount of Cu-MOF, the mechanical properties of the CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite are further improved. When the amount of Cu-MOF increases to \(7\,\mathrm{wt}\%\) , the tensile strength and elongation at break of the CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite reach \(49.2\;\mathrm{MPa}\) and \(2.9\%\) , respectively, which are significantly increased by about \(38.6\%\) and \(81.3\%\) as compared to neat CA. The results suggest that the flower-like Cu-MOF is a highly effective reinforcing agent for CA matrix. Flower-like Cu-MOF possesses a very high surface area and unique surface structure, which is beneficial to absorb and disperse

impact energy [21,38]. Moreover, the well dispersion of Cu-MOF nanoparticles (as seen in Fig. 3) and the good interfacial adhesion between Cu-MOF and CA suppress phase separation, which can ensures effective stress transfer at the interface and delays the failure of CA nanocomposite films during tensile process. Thus, incorporation of flower-like Cu-MOF nanoparticles into the CA matrix can dramatically improve the mechanical properties of the films. Similar reinforcing effects were also observed in other CA-based nanocomposite films, such as CA/graphene and CA/cellulose nanofiber composite films. For instance, the tensile strength value reported by Zhang et al. [39] increased from 2.4 to \(16.3\;\mathrm{MPa}\) (6.8 times) with \(20\,\mathrm{wt}\%\) cellulose nanofibrils loading compared with the case of the CA film. Liu et al. [40] reported that incorporation of \(0.25\,\mathrm{wt}\%\) graphene into CA matrix increased the tensile strength of nanocomposite by \(31.8\%\) compared to the non-modified film. By comparing with relevant results, it is obvious that the reinforcing effect of Cu-MOF on the mechanical properties of CA is much comparable to that of graphene. The improved mechanical property of the CA/ Cu-MOF nanocomposite makes it more promising for practical application.

# 3.3. Thermal properties

DSC and TGA tests were carried out to characterize the thermal properties of CA and CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite films. From the DSC data in Table 1, it is obvious that addition of Cu-MOF reduces the glass transition temperature \((T_{\mathrm{g}})\) of the CA films. The \(T_{\mathrm{g}}\) values decline from \(188.2\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for CA to \(179.1\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(\mathsf{C A}/\mathsf{C u-M O F}^{7.0}\) . A similar result of a decrease in the value of \(T_{\mathrm{g}}\) was observed when organoclay (Cloisite30B) was filled in the CA matrix [41]. The remarkable decline of \(T_{\mathrm{g}}\) is

