associated with the enhanced mobility of the polymer segment and indicates that the addition of Cu-MOF restricts the formation of CA associate, consequently reducing the \( T_{\mathrm{g}} \) value of the CA [19,42]. Meanwhile, it can be found that the melting temperatures \( \left(T_{\mathrm{m}}\right) \) of the CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite films are also decreased as compared with that of pure CA. This can be interpreted by the phase equilibrium theory in which Cu-MOF performs as an impurity to diminish the melting point [38].

The typical TGA and relevant DTG curves of Cu-MOF, CA and CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite films are displayed in Fig. 5. For Cu-MOF, the TGA/ DTG curves present two major weight losses observed at about \( 221\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) and \( 385\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \), respectively, which should be related with the decomposition of 2MI ligand and the final generation of CuO [29]. For neat CA, the TGA/DTG curves exhibit a one-step profile of weight loss, which mainly occurs in the range of \( 220{-}400\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) and can be attributed to the decomposition of the acetyl groups and backbones of cellulose [19,43]. As for CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite films, the pattern of their thermal decomposition is similar to that of neat CA film. From TGA/DTG curves, the onset degradation temperature \( \left(T_{\mathrm{onset}}\right) \) and the maximum degradation temperature \( \left(T_{\mathrm{max}}\right) \) were obtained and listed in Table 1. It is obvious that addition of Cu-MOF reduces the \( T_{\mathrm{onset}} \) of CA film, and the \( T_{\mathrm{onset}} \) tends to decrease with increasing the content of Cu-MOF. For example, the \( T_{\mathrm{onset}} \) is \( 299\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) for the neat CA film, but it decreases to 297, 274, 251 and \( 250\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) when 1, 3, 5, and \( 7\,\mathrm{wt\%} \) of Cu-MOF is incorporated, respectively. This is mainly attributed to the lower thermal stability exhibited by the 2MI ligand in Cu-MOF (Fig. 5). However, the \( T_{\mathrm{max}} \) value for CA film is not much affected by the incorporation of the Cu-MOF, indicating that incorporation of Cu-MOF helps in keeping the thermal decomposition pattern for CA/Cu-MOF films as same as neat CA (Fig. 5b). A recent study reported by Lucena et al. [44], found that incorporation of boric acid or ammonium polyphosphate caused a distinct decrease in value

of \( T_{\mathrm{max}} \) for CA films. Similarly, significant reduction in \( T_{\mathrm{max}} \) value for CA containing carbon nanotubes was also observed as compared to neat CA [45]. The comparison of our TGA results with those reported in recent literatures reveals the beneficial effect of adding Cu-MOF into the CA matrix.

# 3.4. Surface wettability and water vapor permeability

To investigate the effect of Cu-MOF on wettability of CA film, the water contact angles were measured using sessile drop method, and the results are shown in Fig. 6a. Neat CA has a contact angle of \( 66.2^{\circ} \) exhibiting the hydrophilic nature, which is in consistent with the previous reports [46]. The incorporation of Cu-MOF into the CA matrix results in a distinct increase of water contact angle, and the increase is dependent on the content of Cu-MOF. This might be ascribed to the more hydrophobic Cu-MOF compared with the CA matrix. A similar result of an increase in water contact angle was observed when functionalized halloysite nanotubes (AT-Hal/ZnONP) was added into the alginate matrix [47]. Note that the water contact angle of nanocomposite film reaches to a maximum of \( 78.1^{\circ} \) with addition of \( 7\,\mathrm{wt\%} \) of Cu-MOF.

The WVP of the neat CA and CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite films is presented in Fig. 6b. The WVP of neat CA films is \( 8.86 \times 10^{-7}\,\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{Pa}^{-1} \), and it dramatically drops to \( 5.82 \times 10^{-7}\,\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{h}^{-1}\cdot\mathrm{Pa}^{-1} \) of CA/Cu-MOF [7,8]. Namely, CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite films possess much better water vapor barrier ability than neat CA. The decline in the WVP of CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite films is due to the reduced hydrophilicity of the films as evidenced by the water contact angle results (Fig. 6a). The higher the content of Cu-MOF is, the more the WVP of the nanocomposite films drop. On the other hand, the well-dispersed flower-like Cu-MOF nanospheres

