# 3.5. Transparency and UV-shielding properties of CA/Cu-MOF films

Fig. 7a shows the digital images of neat CA and CA/Cu-MOF films. It can be observed that both neat CA and CA/Cu-MOF films are visually uniform and compact transparency, indicating good dispersion of Cu-MOF in the CA matrix. Addition of the Cu-MOF into the CA matrix leads to a light brown color for the nanocomposite film owing to the color of Cu-MOF, and the film color gently deepens with increasing the content of Cu-MOF. The UV–vis absorption and transmittance spectra of neat CA and CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite films are present in Fig. 7b and c. As seen, neat CA presents a very slight absorption in UV region \(250{-}400\,\mathrm{nm}\). Upon Cu-MOF addition, there is a dramatic increase of UV absorption in this region, even the content is \(1.0\,\mathrm{wt\%}\). This signifies flower-like Cu-MOF nanoparticles endow the films with excellent UV shielding ability in the whole UV region \(250{-}400\,\mathrm{nm}\). Similar results were also observed when inorganic oxide nanoparticles (such as \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) [18], \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\) [51]) were incorporated into the CA films. Specifically, the CA nanocomposite films containing \(1.0\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) Cu-MOF can block about \(70.2\%\) of UVC \(250{-}280\,\mathrm{nm}\), \(50.3\%\) of UVB \(280{-}315\,\mathrm{nm}\), and \(19.2\%\) of UVA \(315{-}400\,\mathrm{nm}\). With increasing Cu-MOF content, the UV-shielding capability of the CA/Cu-MOF films is further enhanced. As increasing Cu-MOF content to \(7\,\mathrm{wt\%}\), approximately \(91.8\%\) of UVC, \(93.2\%\) of UVB and \(93.6\%\) of UVA can be blocked by the CA/Cu-MOF films. Simultaneously, it can be found that all the CA/Cu-MOF films still retain a high visible transparency, transmitting about \(64\%\) - \(95\%\) of the radiation at \(700\,\mathrm{nm}\). Thus, incorporation of Cu-MOF into the CA matrix can significantly improve the UV-shielding performance of the films, and the resulting transparent nanocomposite films have great potential for UV-protective application.

The photostability of UV-shielding performance against UV light irradiation is a key factor to influence the long-term application of the UV-protective materials [33,52]. In order to assess the photostability of CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite films, the as-prepared films were exposed to strong UV radiation. Fig. 8 exhibits UV–vis transmittance curves of CA/Cu-MOF films before and after continuous UV irradiation for \(12\,\mathrm{h}\). As seen from Fig. 8, after \(12\,\mathrm{h}\) of UV irradiation, no obvious change can be observed in transmittance spectra for all the sample films, meaning that the irradiated nanocomposite films can still

