maintain the original high transparency and UV-shielding ability. This result indicates the good photostability of CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite films.

In addition, the UV-shielding ability of neat CA and CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite films was further assessed by photocatalytic degradation of RhB solution in the existence of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) nanoparticles. As displayed in Fig. 9, the CA-protected RhB solution exhibits a loss of \(63\%\) after UV irradiation for \(60\,\mathrm{min}\), whereas the RhB solution shielded by CA/Cu\(\mathsf{M O F}^{3.0}\) shows a decrease of \(20\%\). When CA/Cu-MOF7.0 is served as the shielding film, only about \(9\%\) of RhB degrades, demonstrating the outstanding UV-shielding ability of CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite films. These above UV stability and protection experiments reveal that incorporation of Cu-MOF to CA can distinctly improve the UV stability and UV-shielding performance of the films.

This work is focused on the application of flower-like Cu-MOF as a nano-filler to develop polymeric nanocomposites with improved optical properties. Within this context, various inorganic oxide nanoparticles such as ZnO, \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{Al}_{2}0_{3}\), have been filled into transparent polymers to make functional materials with enhanced UV-blocking performance [53–55]. However, incomplete UV absorption of those nanoparticles and their photocatalytic effect on polymer matrix would not be conducive to their application [56]. By contrast, incorporation of flower-like Cu-MOF into the CA matrix results in the nanocomposite films with outstanding photostability and UV-shielding performance in the whole UV region, indicating that Cu-MOF can be used as a good UV-absorber for fabrication of UV-protection nanocomposite films. The well-dispersed Cu-MOF in the CA matrix can effectively shift its UV absorption to cover the whole UV region, and simultaneously improve the UV absorption capability of the films (Fig. 7b). Moreover, Cu-MOF in the hybrids has a very high surface area and unique flower-like structure, which is also in favor of the inevitable reflection and absorption of UV light. Hence, the UV-shielding ability of CA nanocomposite films can be significantly enhanced by the incorporated flower-like Cu-MOF.

# 3.6. Cytotoxicity of CA/Cu-MOF films

The cytotoxicity of neat CA and CA/Cu-MOF nanocomposite films was evaluated using normal human liver cells (HL-7702) by MTT assays, and the results are shown in Fig. 10. It is found that neat CA shows no toxicity as the cell viability is up to \(100\%\). Upon addition of Cu-MOF in CA matrix, the cell viability is gradually decreased with increasing Cu-MOF content, and decreases to about \(80\%\) with the content of \(7\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) Cu-MOF. Typically, cell viability of over \(70\%\) is considered as a standard of nontoxicity of biomaterial samples [57]. It is noted that cell viability of
